</a></div></div></section><section class="py-16 bg-white border-b border-slate-200"><div class="container mx-auto px-4 max-w-5xl"><div class="bg-slate-50 border border-slate-200 p-8 md:p-12 rounded-3xl flex flex-col md:flex-row items-center gap-10 shadow-xl relative overflow-hidden"><div class="absolute -right-10 -top-10 text-9xl text-slate-100 opacity-50"><i class="fas fa-quote-right"></i></div><div class="w-40 h-40 bg-sky-100 rounded-full border-4 border-sky-500 flex items-center justify-center flex-shrink-0 z-10 shadow-[0_0_30px_rgba(14,165,233,0.1)]">
<i class="fas fa-user-tie text-6xl text-sky-600"></i></div><div class="text-center md:text-left z-10"><h3 class="text-3xl font-black text-slate-900 uppercase tracking-wide mb-2">Mehfuz Shahid Khan</h3><p class="text-sky-600 font-bold text-sm uppercase tracking-widest mb-4">Director &#038; Lead HVAC Architect</p><p class="text-slate-700 leading-relaxed font-medium mb-4 text-lg">
&#8220;For over two decades, Atlas Aircon has protected Vadodara&#8217;s homes and heavy industries from catastrophic thermal failures. The HVAC industry is plagued by untrained mechanics who &#8216;top-up&#8217; gas without fixing the leak, or bypass essential safety sensors. We operate differently. We are an engineering firm.&#8221;</p><p class="text-slate-600 text-sm leading-relaxed">
&#8220;Every technician on the Atlas team is strictly vetted, highly trained, and armed with the best digital manifold gauges, vacuum pumps, and multimeters in the state. We don&#8217;t guess. We diagnose, we isolate the fault, and we engineer a permanent solution.&#8221;</p></div></div></div></section><section class="py-24 bg-slate-50"><div class="container mx-auto px-4 max-w-4xl"><div class="text-center mb-16">
<span class="text-sky-600 font-black tracking-widest text-sm uppercase mb-3 block">Engineering Knowledge Base</span><h2 class="text-4xl font-black text-slate-900 uppercase">Frequently Asked Technical Questions</h2><p class="text-slate-600 font-medium mt-4 text-lg">Understanding the mechanics of your cooling system can save you thousands in unnecessary repairs and replacements.</p></div><div class="space-y-6"><div class="bg-white border border-slate-200 p-8 rounded-2xl shadow-sm hover:border-sky-300 hover:shadow-lg transition duration-300"><h4 class="text-xl font-black text-slate-900 mb-4"><i class="fas fa-question-circle text-sky-500 mr-2"></i> Why is my AC running but not blowing cold air?</h4><p class="text-slate-700 text-base leading-relaxed">
This is the most common emergency call we receive. If the indoor blower is running but the air is warm, it usually points to three faults: First, a blown start capacitor in the outdoor unit, meaning the heavy compressor cannot ignite. Second, a severe refrigerant gas leak (usually caused by a rusted U-bend in the condenser coil). Third, a choked capillary tube or expansion valve. Do not keep the AC running in this state, as it can cause the compressor to permanently seize. Disconnect the power and call Atlas Aircon for immediate AC repair in Vadodara.</p></div><div class="bg-white border border-slate-200 p-8 rounded-2xl shadow-sm hover:border-sky-300 hover:shadow-lg transition duration-300"><h4 class="text-xl font-black text-slate-900 mb-4"><i class="fas fa-question-circle text-sky-500 mr-2"></i> What is the difference between R32, R410a, and R22 gas?</h4><p class="text-slate-700 text-base leading-relaxed">
Refrigerants are not interchangeable. Older ACs use R22 (Freon), which is heavily ozone-depleting and is currently being phased out globally, making it very expensive to refill. R410a is a blended synthetic gas used in early Inverter ACs; it requires charging strictly in a liquid state. R32 is the modern standard—it is highly efficient, has a low Global Warming Potential (GWP), but operates at extremely high pressures and is mildly flammable. Atlas engineers strictly use digital weighing scales to charge R32 to the exact gram specified by the manufacturer.</p></div><div class="bg-white border border-slate-200 p-8 rounded-2xl shadow-sm hover:border-sky-300 hover:shadow-lg transition duration-300"><h4 class="text-xl font-black text-slate-900 mb-4"><i class="fas fa-question-circle text-sky-500 mr-2"></i> Why does my Inverter AC PCB keep failing?</h4><p class="text-slate-700 text-base leading-relaxed">
Unlike standard ACs that just turn on and off, Inverter ACs use a complex Printed Circuit Board (PCB) to convert AC power to DC, allowing the compressor to run at variable speeds. Because the electrical grid in Gujarat often experiences sudden voltage spikes, the sensitive microprocessors, IPM modules, and communication ICs on these boards can fry. While company authorized centers will force you to buy a Rs. 8,000 replacement board, Atlas Aircon has the specialized diagnostic tools to repair the burnt components on your existing circuit board at a fraction of the cost.</p></div><div class="bg-white border border-slate-200 p-8 rounded-2xl shadow-sm hover:border-sky-300 hover:shadow-lg transition duration-300"><h4 class="text-xl font-black text-slate-900 mb-4"><i class="fas fa-question-circle text-sky-500 mr-2"></i> Why is vacuuming necessary before gas charging?</h4><p class="text-slate-700 text-base leading-relaxed">
This is the #1 difference between a professional engineer and a cheap local mechanic. Atmospheric air contains moisture. If a mechanic just hooks up a gas cylinder and forces gas into an empty AC, that moisture reacts with the synthetic compressor oil to create hydrofluoric acid. This acid literally eats your compressor from the inside out, destroying it within a year. At Atlas Aircon, we attach a heavy-duty dual-stage vacuum pump for 30 to 45 minutes to boil off all internal moisture and create a pure vacuum before injecting a single drop of new refrigerant during any AC service.</p></div><div class="bg-white border border-slate-200 p-8 rounded-2xl shadow-sm hover:border-sky-300 hover:shadow-lg transition duration-300"><h4 class="text-xl font-black text-slate-900 mb-4"><i class="fas fa-question-circle text-sky-500 mr-2"></i> How often should a commercial Chiller or VRF system be serviced?</h4><p class="text-slate-700 text-base leading-relaxed">
Heavy industrial chillers and commercial VRF (Variable Refrigerant Flow) systems in GIDC areas should undergo mandatory preventative maintenance every 30 to 45 days. Dust accumulation on the massive outdoor heat exchangers exponentially increases the electrical load on the scroll compressors. Furthermore, water-cooled chillers require chemical descaling of their condenser tubes at least twice a year to prevent calcium buildup, which acts as an insulator and destroys cooling efficiency. Our Annual Maintenance Contracts (AMC) handle this scheduling automatically.</p></div></div></div></section><section class="py-20 bg-white border-t border-slate-200"><div class="container mx-auto px-4 max-w-6xl"><div class="text-center mb-10"><h2 class="text-3xl md:text-4xl font-black text-slate-900 uppercase">Live Engineering Logs in Vadodara</h2><p class="text-slate-600 font-medium mt-3 max-w-2xl mx-auto text-lg">Do not trust aggregators who hide their work. Below are real-time, verified dispatches executed by our team right here in the local area.</p></div><style>.atlas-omni-hub { background: #0f172a; border-radius: 16px; padding: 35px; border: 1px solid #1e293b; box-shadow: 0 20px 40px rgba(0,0,0,0.4); font-family: 'Inter', sans-serif; margin: 40px 0; }
